 server pushes to a connected client; change notifications tell clients that a list or a resource they cached is stale.\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:301:// SDK caches the inputSchema and can mirror), then make the runner-supplied\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:779: * schemas are compiled lazily on the first `callTool()` against the cached\n./docs/advanced/gateway.md:44:  cacheScope: 'private',\n./docs/advanced/gateway.md:58:The value is plain JSON. Write the string to Redis, a config map, or a process-local cache; parse it back wherever a client needs it.\n./docs/advanced/gateway.md:157:- The value is plain JSON: stringify it into a shared cache, parse it in any process that fronts the same server.\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:856:- **Worker boundaries** — `structuredClone`/`postMessage` drop the (symbol-keyed) brand,\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1542:  verbs on top of it — `callTool()` and the cacheable list verbs — perform async work\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1543:  first (header-mirroring scan, response-cache freshness, output-validator resolution),\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1599:  `callTool()` against the cached `tools/list` entry, not eagerly inside `listTools()`.\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1604:- On a 2026-07-28 connection the cacheable verbs honour the server-stamped `ttlMs` /\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1605:  `cacheScope` (SEP-2549) and may return a still-fresh cached entry without a round\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1606:  trip. Per-call override: `{ cacheMode: 'refresh' | 'bypass' }`. New `ClientOptions`:\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1607:  `cachePartition`, `defaultCacheTtlMs`. `ResponseCacheStore` gained `delete(key)`;\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1660:- **`registerResource` reserves the `cacheHint` config key.** It is validated\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1663:  smuggled a `cacheHint` key through resource metadata should rename it.\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:33:- [Cache fields and cache hints](#cache-fields-and-cache-hints)\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:367:- **`DiscoverResult` hides its cache fields at the type level only.** `ttlMs` /\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:368:  `cacheScope` on `server/discover` are read by the client's response-cache layer and\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:603:## Cache fields and cache hints\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:605:The 2026-07-28 revision requires `ttlMs` and `cacheScope` on the cacheable results.\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:607:and `cacheScope: 'private'` (the most conservative policy). To advertise a real cache\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:608:policy, set `ServerOptions.cacheHints` (per-operation) or `cacheHint` on a\n./docs/clients/caching.md:6:Caching is one feature with two halves: the server marks a result with a freshness hint, and the client's **response cache** serves it locally while it stays fresh.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:8:## Let the cache work\n./docs/clients/caching.md:10:The cacheable verbs check the cache before they send. A still-fresh entry comes back without a round trip; `cacheMode` overrides the disposition per call.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:12:```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/clients/caching.examples.ts#responseCache_use\"\n./docs/clients/caching.md:13:const tools = await client.listTools(); // network, then cached for the server's ttlMs\n./docs/clients/caching.md:14:const again = await client.listTools(); // served from cache while still fresh\n./docs/clients/caching.md:16:await client.listTools(undefined, { cacheMode: 'refresh' }); // always refetch and re-store\n./docs/clients/caching.md:17:await client.readResource({ uri: 'config://app' }, { cacheMode: 'bypass' }); // no cache read or write\n./docs/clients/caching.md:27:Nothing on the client opts in: every `Client` holds a response cache, and the server's hint decides what it may serve.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:31:`ServerOptions.cacheHints` attaches a `ttlMs` and a `cacheScope` to each cacheable result it names (SEP-2549) — without one, the SDK emits `ttlMs: 0` and no client ever serves that result from cache.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:33:```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/clients/caching.examples.ts#cacheHints_server\"\n./docs/clients/caching.md:37:        cacheHints: {\n./docs/clients/caching.md:38:            'tools/list': { ttlMs: 60_000, cacheScope: 'public' },\n./docs/clients/caching.md:39:            'resources/read': { ttlMs: 5_000, cacheScope: 'private' }\n./docs/clients/caching.md:45:`registerResource` also takes a per-resource `cacheHint`; it wins, field by field, over the `resources/read` entry here for that resource's read results. Mark a result `cacheScope: 'public'` only when it is identical for every caller — anything derived from the caller's authorization context stays `'private'`, the default.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:51:## Choose a cache mode per call\n./docs/clients/caching.md:53:`cacheMode` on `listTools()`, `listPrompts()`, `listResources()`, `listResourceTemplates()`, and `readResource()` — the cacheable verbs — takes one of three values. `'use'`, the default, serves a still-fresh entry and otherwise fetches and stores. `'refresh'` always fetches and stores the fresh result.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:55:`'bypass'` fetches without reading or writing: it leaves the cache byte-untouched, including the `tools/list` entry the SDK itself reads for output validation when you [call tools](./calling.md).\n./docs/clients/caching.md:59:`responseCacheStore` swaps the backing store; the default is a fresh `InMemoryResponseCacheStore` per client, holding at most 512 `resources/read` entries.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:61:```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/clients/caching.examples.ts#responseCacheStore_shared\"\n./docs/clients/caching.md:64:const client = new Client({ name: 'my-client', version: '1.0.0' }, { responseCacheStore: store });\n./docs/clients/caching.md:71:When one shared store serves several principals, set `cachePartition` to a stable identity of the authorization context — the auth subject, for example.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:73:```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/clients/caching.examples.ts#cachePartition_perUser\"\n./docs/clients/caching.md:74:const client = new Client({ name: 'gateway', version: '1.0.0' }, { responseCacheStore: sharedStore, cachePartition: userId });\n./docs/clients/caching.md:80:A shared store without `cachePartition` can serve one user's `'private'`-scoped resource bodies to another. Set it whenever the store outlives a single principal.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:85:`defaultCacheTtlMs` is the TTL applied when a cacheable result arrives without a `ttlMs`. The default is `0`: a result with no hint is never served from cache.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:91:Fresh or not, the cache also evicts itself when the server signals a change: a `list_changed` notification drops the matching list entries, and `notifications/resources/updated` drops the cached body for that URI — see [Subscriptions](./subscriptions.md).\n./docs/clients/caching.md:99:- Caching is one feature with two halves: the server attaches `ttlMs` / `cacheScope`, the client honours them — by default neither half does anything alone.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:100:- `listTools()`, `listPrompts()`, `listResources()`, `listResourceTemplates()`, and `readResource()` serve a still-fresh result without a round trip; `cacheMode` overrides per call.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:101:- A result without a hint carries `ttlMs: 0` and is never served from cache; the client caps every `ttlMs` at 24 hours.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:102:- `responseCacheStore` swaps the backing store; `cachePartition` is mandatory when that store serves several principals.\n./examples/README.md:44:| [`caching/`](./caching/README.md)                                   | `cacheHints` stamping on cacheable results (2026-07-28)                                                                                                     | stdio + http | modern         |\n./examples/gateway/client.ts:8: *    you would write this to Redis, a config map, or a process-local cache).\n./examples/gateway/client.ts:49:// map / a process-local cache here. JSON round-trips by design.\n./examples/gateway/README.md:19:const persisted = JSON.stringify(bootstrap.getDiscoverResult()); // → write to Redis / config / process-local cache\n./examples/gateway/README.md:42:- A horizontally-scaled host where one worker's probe should seed the fleet (persist the blob to a shared cache).\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:1847:        // List tools to cache the schemas\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:1923:        // List tools to cache the schemas\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:1995:        // List tools to cache the schemas\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2063:        // List tools to cache the schemas\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2159:        // List tools to cache the schemas\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2240:        // List tools to cache the schemas\n./examples/todos-server/todos.ts:659:            // Priorities are board-visible state — watchers and list caches must hear about it.\n./examples/caching/server.ts:4: * The 2026-07-28 revision requires `ttlMs`/`cacheScope` on the cacheable\n./examples/caching/server.ts:9: *   2. a per-registration `cacheHint` (here: the resource's read result),\n./examples/caching/server.ts:10: *   3. the server-level per-operation `ServerOptions.cacheHints`,\n./examples/caching/server.ts:11: *   4. the conservative defaults (`ttlMs: 0`, `cacheScope: 'private'`).\n./examples/caching/server.ts:25:// cache-served call never reached the server.\n./examples/caching/server.ts:35:            cacheHints: {\n./examples/caching/server.ts:36:                'resources/list': { ttlMs: 5000, cacheScope: 'public' },\n./examples/caching/server.ts:37:                'tools/list': { ttlMs: 30_000, cacheScope: 'public' }\n./examples/caching/server.ts:50:            cacheHint: { ttlMs: 60_000, cacheScope: 'private' }\n./examples/caching/server.ts:58:    // A tool, so tools/list has something to cache.\n./examples/caching/server.ts:62:    // can assert that a cache-served call did not reach the wire.\n./examples/caching/server.ts:73:    // cache-served `listTools()` never reached the wire. `McpServer` registers\n./examples/caching/client.ts:2: * Reads the cache hints emitted on cacheable results (2026-07-28 connections\n./examples/caching/client.ts:3: * only) and asserts the client honours them: a still-fresh cached entry is\n./examples/caching/client.ts:12:    cacheScope?: 'public' | 'private';\n./examples/caching/client.ts:33:// The server stamps `tools/list` with `ttlMs: 30_000, cacheScope: 'public'`.\n./examples/caching/client.ts:36:check.equal(tools.cacheScope, 'public');\n./examples/caching/client.ts:40:// The second call is served from the response cache: the server-side\n./examples/caching/client.ts:42:// held entry (so mutating it cannot reach the cache).\n./examples/caching/client.ts:50:// `cacheMode: 'refresh'` always fetches and re-stores: the counter moves.\n./examples/caching/client.ts:51:await client.listTools(undefined, { cacheMode: 'refresh' });\n./examples/caching/client.ts:56:check.equal(resources.cacheScope, 'public');\n./examples/caching/client.ts:62:check.equal(read.cacheScope, 'private');\n./examples/caching/client.ts:65:// Within TTL, default `cacheMode: 'use'` → served from cache; the server\n./examples/caching/client.ts:70:// `cacheMode: 'refresh'` always fetches and re-stores.\n./examples/caching/client.ts:71:await client.readResource({ uri: 'config://app' }, { cacheMode: 'refresh' });\n./examples/caching/client.ts:74:// After the refresh the entry is fresh again — back to cache-served.\n./examples/caching/package.json:21:        \"//\": \"cacheHints (ttlMs / cacheScope on cacheable results) are emitted only toward 2026-era clients.\"\n./examples/caching/README.md:3:`CacheableResult` freshness hints (protocol revision 2026-07-28). The server declares hints at two layers — a per-registration `cacheHint` on the resource and server-level `ServerOptions.cacheHints` — and the SDK resolves most-specific-author-first (handler-return fields would\n./examples/caching/README.md:4:take precedence over both) and stamps `ttlMs`/`cacheScope` on the wire toward modern clients only. The client honours the stamped values: a still-fresh held entry is served without a round trip.\n./examples/caching/README.md:10:The client calls `listTools()` and `readResource()` twice each; the second of each pair is served from the response cache. The server exposes a `request-count` tool (how many `tools/list` requests reached it) and a `read-count` tool (how many times the resource handler ran), so the example asserts each counter is unchanged after the cache-served call and increments after `cacheMode: 'refresh'`.\n./examples/caching/README.md:12:## `cacheMode`\n./examples/caching/README.md:14:Per-call control on the cacheable verbs (`listTools()` / `listPrompts()` / `listResources()` / `listResourceTemplates()` / `readResource()`):\n./examples/caching/README.md:17:await client.readResource({ uri: 'config://app' }); // 'use' (default): serve from cache if fresh\n./examples/caching/README.md:18:await client.readResource({ uri: 'config://app' }, { cacheMode: 'refresh' }); // always fetch, then re-store\n./examples/caching/README.md:19:await client.readResource({ uri: 'config://app' }, { cacheMode: 'bypass' }); // fetch; do not read or write the cache\n./examples/caching/README.md:26:The default per-client `InMemoryResponseCacheStore` (bounded at 512 entries by default) is enough for most hosts. To back the cache with something persistent (Redis, KV, IndexedDB), implement the five-method `ResponseCacheStore` interface — the store is a dumb keyed-value carrier; freshness and partitioning are the client's job:\n./examples/caching/README.md:49:const client = new Client({ name: 'host', version: '1.0.0' }, { responseCacheStore: new MyStore(), cachePartition: principalId });\n./examples/caching/README.md:52:The SDK scopes every entry by the connected server's identity automatically — you do not encode server identity into `cachePartition` or the store key yourself. When one store backs several principals against the same server, set `ClientOptions.cachePartition` to a stable identity of the authorization context (e.g. the auth subject) so `'private'`-scoped entries are isolated per principal; `'public'`-scoped entries are shared within the connected server's namespace automatically. Note `serverInfo` is self-reported, so a server that deliberately impersonates another's `name`/`version` shares its `'public'` slot; the per-principal isolation holds regardless.\n./examples/guides/servers/input-required.examples.ts:129:    'wipe-cache',\n./examples/guides/servers/input-required.examples.ts:140:                            message: 'Really wipe the cache?',\n./examples/guides/servers/input-required.examples.ts:200:    'Really wipe the cache?': { confirm: true },\n./examples/guides/servers/input-required.examples.ts:230:console.log(await client.callTool({ name: 'wipe-cache', arguments: {} }));\n./examples/guides/servers/input-required.examples.ts:238:        params: { name: 'wipe-cache', arguments: {}, requestState: 'tampered' }\n./examples/cli-client/README.md:74:| Resources          | `@todos:todos://board` in a message, `/resources` | `resources/read` → injected as a provenance-labelled context block; `list_changed` keeps the cached list fresh                                                         |\n./examples/cli-client/script/session.ts:320:                    'resources/list_changed should have refreshed the cached list'\n./examples/cli-client/cli.ts:87:// Tab completion needs the host's cached lists, but the host needs the UI — resolve lazily.\n./